WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.855
Next.js applications can render and fetch
data in several ways.

2
00:00:05.935 --> 00:00:10.001
Some work happens on the server for each
request.

3
00:00:10.081 --> 00:00:13.651
Some output may be generated ahead of
time.

4
00:00:13.731 --> 00:00:18.293
Some behavior runs in the browser after
the page loads.

5
00:00:18.373 --> 00:00:23.828
Some components or handlers may execute
only in server contexts,

6
00:00:23.908 --> 00:00:29.065
while client components are delivered to
the user's browser.

7
00:00:29.145 --> 00:00:35.988
These distinctions are security-relevant
because secrets, trusted decisions,

8
00:00:36.068 --> 00:00:41.125
and sensitive data belong on the server
side of the boundary.

9
00:00:41.205 --> 00:00:46.263
Data crossing from server to browser
should be intentional.

10
00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:51.698
Anything sent to the client can be
inspected, copied, modified,

11
00:00:51.778 --> 00:00:53.563
or replayed by a user.

12
00:00:53.643 --> 00:00:58.007
That does not make the browser hostile in
every case,

13
00:00:58.087 --> 00:01:04.434
but it means the browser is not a place to
keep server-only secrets or enforce

14
00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:06.795
sensitive authorization.

15
00:01:06.875 --> 00:01:11.238
A component can hide a field or button for
usability,

16
00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:17.963
but the server still needs to decide
whether the current identity may access the

17
00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:20.324
data or perform the action.

18
00:01:20.404 --> 00:01:24.371
Build-time behavior also deserves
attention.

19
00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:31.690
Static generation and build outputs can
accidentally include data that was available

20
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:35.539
during a build but should not be public
later.

21
00:01:35.619 --> 00:01:42.759
Runtime rendering can accidentally return
authorization-dependent data under the

22
00:01:42.839 --> 00:01:44.426
wrong cache scope.

23
00:01:44.506 --> 00:01:47.679
Middleware can shape requests early,

24
00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:54.106
but it should not become the only place
where business authorization lives.

25
00:01:54.186 --> 00:02:00.930
Each rendering and routing path should
have a clear explanation of what it trusts

26
00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:02.696
and what it exposes.

27
00:02:02.776 --> 00:02:08.924
The durable principle is simple: know
where code runs before trusting it.

28
00:02:09.004 --> 00:02:13.467
Browser code is for user interaction,
presentation,

29
00:02:13.547 --> 00:02:16.621
and calls back to trusted endpoints.

30
00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:23.048
Server code is where secrets, privileged
data access, session validation,

31
00:02:23.128 --> 00:02:26.699
and authorization decisions should live.

32
00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:33.423
Framework behavior and platform
configuration can support these boundaries,

33
00:02:33.503 --> 00:02:38.660
but application logic must still enforce
them deliberately.
